include "../../linux.mereo"

-- A path held in a NAMED literal, which is the case that used to be broken.
--
-- The kernel's path arguments carry no length -- `openat`, `statx`, `unlinkat`
-- and nine more read to the first zero byte -- so a literal without a
-- terminator walked into whatever the frame held next. `/etc/hostname` became
-- `/etc/hostnameXXXX...` and the open failed with -ENOENT, and WHICH failure
-- depended on the neighbouring declaration. Writing the same path inline
-- worked, because that form lowers to a C string literal and C supplies the
-- zero. Two spellings of one thing, one of them broken.
--
-- Every string literal now allocates one byte more than it counts. `.size` is
-- unchanged -- the terminator is storage, not value -- so nothing that carries
-- a length is affected, and the corpus is the same total size as before.
--
-- Run from the repo root: prints the first line of lorem_ipsum.txt.

-- `tail` is declared straight after `name` ON PURPOSE. Without a terminator the
-- path runs into it and becomes `lorem_ipsum.txtXXXX...`, which does not exist.
-- With a zero-filled neighbour the bug hides, which is what makes it nasty: the
-- same program works or fails depending on what is declared next to it.

program goes
  name is "lorem_ipsum.txt"
  tail is "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX"
  block is 16 bytes
  n is 0
  terminal is already linux.file (descriptor is 1)
  source is linux.file (path is name, flags is 0, mode is 0)
  source.read (buffer is block, capacity is 16, count is n)
  terminal.write (buffer is block, count is n)
  terminal.write (buffer is tail, count is 0)
end
